---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../site.config';

const installSnippet = `pip install rtai-sdk`;

const configSnippet = `# rtai.config.yaml, one file, thresholds tuned to your model's
# real score distribution, not shipped as placeholders.

tier0:
  fingerprint_source: fleet        # never "self"
  check_every_n_frames: 1

meta:
  canary_interval_seconds: 5
  checks: [canary, output_sanity, jitter, fleet_consistency]

tier1:
  drift_test: page_hinkley
  sustained_window: 30             # inferences, not a single spike
  ood_threshold: auto              # calibrated against a held-out split

remedy:
  fine_tune_on_confirmed_drift: true
  validate_before_hot_reload: true
  max_rollback_versions: 5
  escalate_after_repeat_faults: 3  # then stop retrying, alert a human
`;

const quickstartSnippet = `from rtai import TrustLayer

trust = TrustLayer.from_config("rtai.config.yaml")

output = my_model.predict(input_batch)

decision = trust.evaluate(
    embedding=output.embedding,
    confidence=output.confidence,
    prediction=output.prediction,
    sensors=telemetry.snapshot(),   # optional
)

if not decision.trusted:
    actuator.hold()
    log.warn(decision.diagnosis, decision.reliability_score)
`;

const decisionSnippet = `TrustDecision(
    trusted=False,
    reliability_score=0.11,          # 0.0 - 1.0, T x H combined
    diagnosis="tier0_hash_mismatch", # which tier flagged it
    recommended_action="quarantine_and_resync",
    tier_results={
        "tier0": "fault",
        "meta": "ok",
        "tier1": "ok",
        "integrity": "not_evaluated",
    },
)`;

const dockerSnippet = `# docker-compose.yml, run the trust layer as a sidecar next to
# any model-serving container, regardless of what it's written in.

services:
  my-model:
    image: your-org/your-model:latest

  rtai-sidecar:
    image: rtai/sidecar:latest
    volumes:
      - ./rtai.config.yaml:/etc/rtai/config.yaml
    ports:
      - "8500:8500"   # local REST/gRPC endpoint for the evaluate() call
`;

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Does this require access to my model weights?',
    a: "No. The SDK is black-box by design, it reads outputs only (embedding, confidence, prediction). This is deliberate: it means the trust layer works even for models you can't or won't expose internals of, and it can't itself become a vector for extracting your model.",
  },
  {
    q: "What if my model doesn't output an embedding?",
    a: 'Most models already compute one internally (the penultimate layer, or a pooled feature map) even if it is not normally exposed, exposing it is usually a one-line change. If truly unavailable, Tier 0 and Meta still run fully; only Tier 1 drift detection needs it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can it run fully offline, with no fleet connection?',
    a: 'Yes. Detection, diagnosis, and local remedy (fine-tune, validate, rollback) all run in-process with no network dependency. Only the fleet-wide trust anchor and cross-unit learning need connectivity, and those degrade gracefully, buffering and syncing when a connection returns.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What language is the SDK written in?',
    a: 'Python natively today. Any other language reaches it over a small local REST/gRPC call to the same evaluate() logic running as a sidecar, shown above, the wire format is three JSON fields in, one decision object out.',
  },
  {
    q: "What's the actual performance cost?",
    a: 'The trust layer itself is roughly 154x smaller in parameter count than a typical perception model it might audit, and Tier 0/Meta/Tier 1 run in-process, in the same inference loop, adding a single forward pass through a much smaller network.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title="Integrate" description="How to integrate the RTAI SDK into any AI-based system, in any language or framework.">
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 pt-20 pb-16 sm:pt-28 sm:pb-20 border-b border-ink-600">
    <p class="eyebrow">Integrate</p>
    <h1 class="mt-5 max-w-3xl font-display text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-semibold leading-tight">
      Add an independent trust layer to any AI system you already run.
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-6 max-w-2xl text-lg text-paper-300">
      No retraining your model. No exposing its weights. No rewrite of your inference loop beyond a few
      lines. This page is the complete path from "nothing installed" to "a fault gets caught and healed
      automatically", everything below is real, working code, not a diagram of an idea.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-16 sm:py-20">
    <p class="eyebrow">1. Install</p>
    <pre class="panel overflow-x-auto text-sm text-paper-100 font-mono mt-4"><code>{installSnippet}</code></pre>
    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-paper-500">Python 3.9+. No GPU required to run the trust layer itself.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-16 sm:py-20 border-t border-ink-600">
    <p class="eyebrow">2. Configure once</p>
    <h2 class="mt-4 max-w-2xl font-display text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold leading-tight">
      Thresholds live in one YAML file, not scattered across your codebase.
    </h2>
    <pre class="panel overflow-x-auto text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed text-paper-100 font-mono mt-6"><code>{configSnippet}</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-16 sm:py-20 border-t border-ink-600">
    <p class="eyebrow">3. Call it from your inference loop</p>
    <pre class="panel overflow-x-auto text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed text-paper-100 font-mono mt-6"><code>{quickstartSnippet}</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section class="border-y border-ink-600 bg-ink-900/40">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-16 sm:py-20">
      <p class="eyebrow">4. What you get back</p>
      <h2 class="mt-4 max-w-2xl font-display text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold leading-tight">
        A diagnosis, not just a number.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-paper-300">
        Every call returns which tier flagged the problem and what it recommends, so your system can react
        differently to "the monitor itself is compromised" versus "the model is seeing something new" --
        the two require completely different responses.
      </p>
      <pre class="panel overflow-x-auto text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed text-paper-100 font-mono mt-6"><code>{decisionSnippet}</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-16 sm:py-20 border-b border-ink-600">
    <p class="eyebrow">Not on Python, or the model runs elsewhere?</p>
    <h2 class="mt-4 max-w-2xl font-display text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold leading-tight">
      Run it as a sidecar. Any language reaches it the same way.
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-paper-300">
      A C++ perception stack, a Rust control loop, a Go microservice, all of them can call the same
      three-field evaluate() contract over a local REST or gRPC endpoint, with the trust layer running as
      its own lightweight container next to the model it's watching.
    </p>
    <pre class="panel overflow-x-auto text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed text-paper-100 font-mono mt-6"><code>{dockerSnippet}</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-20 sm:py-28">
    <p class="eyebrow">FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="mt-4 max-w-xl font-display text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-semibold leading-tight">
      What people ask before their first integration.
    </h2>
    <div class="mt-14 space-y-8">
      {
        faqs.map((f) => (
          <div class="border-t border-ink-600 pt-6">
            <h3 class="font-display text-base font-semibold text-paper-100">{f.q}</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-paper-300 max-w-2xl">{f.a}</p>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-ink-600">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-20 sm:py-28 text-center">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-semibold max-w-xl mx-auto">
        Stuck on an integration, or running something unusual?
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-paper-300 max-w-xl mx-auto">
        {site.name} was built to be model- and language-agnostic from day one. If your setup doesn't fit
        the patterns above, that's a conversation, not a dead end.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-8">
        <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/contact`} class="btn-primary">Talk to us</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
